⇒ To make code ready for production - minify/ bundle/ compress

npm ⇒ manages packages and it is the standard repository for all the packages 

=> creates package.json  and package-lock.json

which manages the versions of the dependencies used.

```jsx
npm init
```

Bundlers (web pack, parcel, vite) ⇒ it packages or bundles our app to be ready to push to production.

ex: create react-app uses webPack

```jsx
npm install -D parcel
```

=> 2 types of dependencies 
 1. devDependencies -D 
 2. normalDependencies(used in production as well)

"react": "^18.2.0" ⇒ for dependencies in package.json it starts with caret or tilde. If we use caret, when there is a minor update, it automatically updates itself. If we use tilde, it updates the major version for the package. It is always better to have caret, as we need to know what updates are given for the latest major version before we install.

package-lock.json keeps track of the exact version of the package used in our app whereas package.json keeps a approx version of it.

** integrity with hash for each package in package-lock.json makes sure the same version is in production as in development

parcel needs its own dependencies - transitive dependencies and each dependent package has its own package.json

** we need not push node_modules to git as we can generate them by npm install from the package.json and package-lock.json(which needs to be pushed to git)

```jsx
npx parcel index.html(SOURCE_FILE)
```

npx is the command to execute 

